WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:01.243
- [Devin] Thanks to Audible

00:00:01.243 --> 00:00:02.853
for keeping LegalEagle in the air.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.363
- Take off the robes, please.

00:00:08.388 --> 00:00:09.530
(attendees laughs)

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.490
- Yeah, so, it is absolutely true

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.000
that they wore judicial robes,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.570
trying to get the ire of Judge Hoffman.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.440
In response, the judge did, in fact,

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.444
issue contempt citations against them.

00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:23.406
(whimsical music)

00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:26.180
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.550
and today, we are covering
"The Trial of the Chicago 7,"

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.150
which was written and
directed by Aaron Sorkin.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.610
Believe it or not, this
is actually a new movie.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.660
And not only is it a new movie,
but it's a new legal movie.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.340
It's like the before times.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.810
So I'm really excited to
take a look at this movie

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.970
that involves one of the most
famous trials in the 1960s.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.657
And yeah, let's get to it.

00:00:48.657 --> 00:00:50.522
(tense music)

00:00:50.522 --> 00:00:52.960
- December 30th.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.760
- [Man] All those whose
birthday falls on December 30th.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.980
- [Reporter] President Johnson announcing

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.929
new monthly draft totals
increasing to 35,000.

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:02.230
- 43,000 per month.
- 51,000.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.440
- Yeah, it's interesting that,
in the public consciousness,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.000
for some reason in modern times,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.700
maybe it's just me, but I
think we tend to associate

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.370
the Vietnam War with Richard Nixon,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.300
but for the most part, it was Johnson

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.010
who really escalated things in Vietnam

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.580
and instituted the draft.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.220
Later on, I assume we're gonna get to

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.840
the actual protests themselves.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.140
Everyone's protesting Johnson
and the Democratic nominees

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.690
because they were the ones
that were largely responsible

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.230
for the escalation in Vietnam

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.810
and that's why everyone got arrested.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.200
Eventually, Nixon comes
in as this president

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.710
that's going to withdraw from Vietnam,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.600
which he eventually
does in his second term,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.290
but for the most part, the
Democrats were the party

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.110
that were pro-Vietnam at the time.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.320
- If America's soul
becomes totally poisoned,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.767
part of the autopsy must read, "Vietnam."

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:48.980
(gun bangs)

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.490
- What we need in the
United States is not hatred,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.791
but is love and wisdom.

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:57.300
So I ask you tonight to return home,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.991
to say a prayer for the
family of Martin Luther King.

00:02:00.991 --> 00:02:01.865
(gun bangs)

00:02:01.865 --> 00:02:03.610
(crowd screams)

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.390
- Yeah, it's also easy to forget

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.420
that Martin Luther King and
Robert Kennedy were shot

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.530
just months apart from each other.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.100
And Kennedy was shot right
around the time that he won.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.158
I think it was the day that
he won the California primary

00:02:17.158 --> 00:02:18.900
for the Democratic nomination.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.460
And it's one of the great
counterfactuals in the world

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.410
to think about what would've happened

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.420
if Robert Kennedy had won
the Democratic nomination

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.240
if he hadn't been
assassinated before that.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.560
When we get to the protests
and the trial itself,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.930
eventually Hubert Humphreys
is the one that's nominated

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.060
for the Democratic
nomination for president

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.350
and he wasn't an anti-war protest.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.183
Historians think that,

00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:42.530
if Humphreys had been an
anti=war candidate in 1968,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.010
that he would have beaten Richard Nixon.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.884
But he wasn't and he lost.

00:02:45.884 --> 00:02:49.230
- Section 2101, Title 18.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.350
That's the federal law that was broken.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.779
- That's the Rap Brown Law.

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:54.750
- In conspiracy to cross state lines

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.410
in order to incite violence.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.640
- Okay.
- It's a maximum of 10 years.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.570
We want all 10.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:00.479
- For whom, sir?

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:01.647
- The all-star team.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:02.537
(file thumps)

00:03:02.537 --> 00:03:04.920
- Okay, so this is really interesting,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.840
and I think this is kind
of a throwaway line.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.350
Schultz here, who is the prosecutor,

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.800
talks about the H. Rap Brown Law.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.920
H. Rap Brown was the chairman

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.920
of the Student Nonviolent
Coordinating Committee.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.500
In 1967, he gave a speech
to a few dozen people

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.370
in Cambridge, Maryland,
and the quote that he said

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.637
was something like, "It's
time for Cambridge to explode.

00:03:26.637 --> 00:03:28.334
We're gonna have to burn America down."

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:30.120
And then the group of people

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.730
exchanged gunfire with the police

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.950
and it's sort of an open
question who started that.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.720
The H. Rap Brown Law was a law

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.730
that was introduced by Strom
Thurmond, who is notorious

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.840
for being a sort of racist
senator from the South.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.460
When the Civil Rights Act 1968 was passed

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:47.730
or was attempted to be passed,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.160
there was obviously
quite a lot of pushback.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.330
At the time, there was
a lot of white fright,

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.290
not only people protesting the war,

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.120
but also what people saw
as outside agitators.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.803
And it was a law that was
meant to prevent people

00:03:58.803 --> 00:04:03.100
from coming out of state and
basically inciting violence.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.930
Often, when you're
dealing with federal laws,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.200
the way that you overcome

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.000
the commerce clause of the US Constitution

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.010
and allow Congress to
actually legislate something

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.540
is you say that, when
you come out of state

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.230
or you cross state lines, then
that's when the law applies.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.644
In order to pass the civil rights law

00:04:17.644 --> 00:04:19.620
along with, I think it was a housing bill,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.900
they had to be passed with this rider

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.200
that was added by Strom Thurmond.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.010
This was 1969 at the time.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.740
No one had ever used this law

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.730
and it was clearly passed
for political reasons.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.580
The fact that Bobby
Seale, the Black Panther,

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.020
was even included in this group of people

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.250
that actually organized
the protests in 1968,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.440
that was politicized.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.180
The law that they were prosecuted under

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.370
was completely politicized.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:41.500
It was...

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.040
It's as close as America
has really ever come

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.576
to a political show trial.

00:04:45.576 --> 00:04:47.380
- [Protesters] The
whole world is watching!

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.024
The whole world is watching!

00:04:49.024 --> 00:04:50.966
The whole world is watching!

00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:53.297
The whole world is watching!
- Lock up the longhairs!

00:04:53.297 --> 00:04:54.921
- [Protesters] The
whole world is watching!

00:04:54.921 --> 00:04:56.743
The whole world is watching!

00:04:56.743 --> 00:04:58.113
The whole world is watching!

00:04:58.113 --> 00:05:01.860
The whole world is watching!
- Okay, so a couple things.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.810
Here, the movie's telling
us this is District Court,

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.390
so it's a federal courthouse
because, like I said,

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.890
we're dealing with a federal law here.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:12.000
This was the H. Rap
Brown Anti-Rioting Act.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.060
On top of it, the chant
that they're chanting here,

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.070
I don't know if that
continued into the trial,

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.005
but famously, when the police
cracked down on the protestors

00:05:20.005 --> 00:05:23.270
at the Democratic Convention,
the protesters themselves,

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.322
as they were basically
being beaten by the police,

00:05:25.322 --> 00:05:27.700
were chanting, "The
whole world is watching.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.079
The whole world is watching."

00:05:29.079 --> 00:05:29.977
- [Protesters] The
whole world is watching!

00:05:29.977 --> 00:05:32.970
- [Woman] Fine, if you
want the street, take it!

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.380
- I assume that that probably continued on

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.740
even after the protest itself,

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.946
but that chant famously originated

00:05:38.946 --> 00:05:41.960
with the protests themselves.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.530
- I want you all to meet a new
addition to the defense team.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.850
This is Leonard Weinglass,
one of this country's

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.180
most talented First Amendment litigators.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.013
- Bill, can you tell us

00:05:52.013 --> 00:05:52.995
the status of-
- Go ahead, Sy.

00:05:52.995 --> 00:05:55.160
- Can you tell us the
status of Charles Garry?

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.900
- Charles Garry is still in the hospital.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.204
You should contact his
office for information.

00:06:01.204 --> 00:06:02.640
Uh, Marjorie.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.610
- Does that mean you're
representing Bobby Seale today?

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:07.573
- Oh, no, it's very important
that it be understood that,

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.100
for his own protection,
his own protection,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.042
I will not be acting as
Bobby Seale's attorney today.

00:06:14.042 --> 00:06:15.230
- All right, so they're hinting

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.770
at a whole bunch of different things here.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.130
Number one is that Kunstler and Weinglass

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.810
were the main trial
counsel for the Chicago 7,

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.070
but also, there were dozens of lawyers

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:24.990
that were involved here.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.520
It was not just these two guys.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:27.670
And they're hinting

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:31.070
that a man named Charles
Garry is not present.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.700
This is a famous episode
in federal litigation

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.173
where a lot of the
members of the Chicago 7,

00:06:37.173 --> 00:06:40.090
they were really the Chicago
8 if you include Bobby Seale,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.900
wanted Charles Garry, a famous
litigator, to represent them.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.070
In particular, Bobby
Seale, the Black Panther,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.480
wanted Charles Garry to represent him.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.370
And he was in the hospital

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.420
because he needed gallbladder surgery,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.027
so he was literally in a hospital bed.

00:06:54.027 --> 00:06:56.270
And so it was a huge question

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.720
as to who was representing
whom at the start of the trial.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.550
- I understand why they're trying to smoke

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.233
Abbie, Jerry, and Hayden,

00:07:04.233 --> 00:07:06.510
even Rennie and Dellinger,
but for the life of me,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.050
I can't figure out what the
two of us are doing here.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.430
- I feel exactly the same way.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.507
But this is the Academy
Awards of protests,

00:07:12.507 --> 00:07:13.820
and as far as I'm concerned,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.670
it's an honor just to be nominated.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:16.880
- Okay, this hints at something else

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.530
that's probably kind of important,

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.920
is that, because this
is kind of a show trial

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.230
and very politicized, the
prosecutors were cramming

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:24.810
a whole bunch of disparate groups

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.610
into the defense table here.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.980
You had the Yippies that
were led by Abbie Hoffman,

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.680
who thought this was all kind of a joke.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.950
You have the SDS, by Tom Hayden,

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.410
who were very, very anti-war.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.825
You have these local
guys, Weiner and Froines,

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:41.660
who were not nationally known figures.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.330
They were just involved
in the local planning,

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.290
but not really the
overarching national campaign.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.240
You've got Bobby Seale, who's the head

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.640
of the Black Panthers at this point.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.090
And then you have David Dellinger,

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.560
who I think is involved with the MOBE,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.310
the, like, student
mobilization against the war.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:59.316
And they're all on trial for
a conspiracy to create a riot.

00:07:59.316 --> 00:08:02.850
Weiner and Froines were
completely the odd men out.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.350
And it's sort of crazy
that all of these people

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.348
were prosecuted at the same time

00:08:07.348 --> 00:08:10.860
when they couldn't have less
to do with each other, really.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.000
They barely agreed

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.240
that everyone should be
against the Vietnam War.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.688
But that's kind of where
all the cooperation ended.

00:08:19.688 --> 00:08:20.993
- [Bailiff] All rise.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.580
- Hmm, okay.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.880
So, if this is actually the
start of the trial itself,

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.860
if we're done with the pre-trial stuff,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.420
then at this point, it was
already sort of a circus

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:39.440
and most, if not all of the defendants,

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.990
refused to stand to recognize the judge,

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:43.380
which was actually a subject

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.990
of a lot of the contempt charges

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:48.610
that Judge Julius Hoffman would hand down

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.940
to a bunch of the defendants,

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.190
especially the Yippies,
with Abbie Hoffman,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.770
were absolutely dead set on making thing

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.840
as wild, as much like a circus,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.800
and showing the least
amount of respect possible.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.580
- Move to reinstate testimony.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.110
- [Defendants] Overruled.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:06.770
- Among the many things that they did,

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.610
they refused to stand for
large portions of the trial.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:15.610
- 69CR180, United States of America versus

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:21.320
David Dellinger, Rennard
C. Davis, Thomas Hayden,

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.810
Abbot Hoffman, Jerry C. Rubin, Lee Weiner,

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:30.170
John R. Froines, and
Bobby G. Seale for trial.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.740
- Are the People ready to
make opening arguments?

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:33.880
- We are, Your Honor.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.190
- I don't have my lawyer here.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.830
- It's not your turn to speak.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.300
- My trial's begun without my lawyer.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:40.133
- Okay.

00:09:40.133 --> 00:09:42.460
So that's Bobby Seale,
the famous Black Panther.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.300
And they're compressing

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.700
a lot of stuff into this statement here

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.970
because we haven't even been able to see

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.310
the pre-trial stuff that
happened before the court.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.518
A lot of stuff that
happens in motion practice,

00:09:53.518 --> 00:09:56.950
between counsel and the judge,
and you're choosing the jury.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.070
There's so many things that
goes into the pre-trial.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.270
And here, it was a huge, huge issue

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:04.310
about who represented Bobby Seale.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.030
By the time that the trial starts,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.070
several of the pre-trial lawyers

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.490
have already been held in contempt

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:13.910
and Judge Hoffman has sent
them to jail without bail,

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:17.110
which caused over a 100 lawyers
to write an amicus brief,

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:20.100
where you're just writing a
brief as a friend of the court.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.750
You're not someone that is actually,

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.820
has a vested interest in the trial itself.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.940
But hundreds of lawyers
wrote in to the judge

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:27.370
and said, "This is crazy.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:30.380
Why are you jailing the lawyers
for the defendants here?"

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.630
And so it was a complete open question

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.510
as to who represented Bobby Seale.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.690
William Kunstler, the main trial
counsel for the defendants,

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.120
explicitly said he was
not representing Seale.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.020
- Mr. Seale is here without
legal representation.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.490
- I don't care for your general tone.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:48.670
- Arguably, Seale was
completely unrepresented

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:51.750
for most of this trial
and was vociferously

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.900
disputing the fact that
they were moving forward

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:55.380
without someone representing him.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.250
I mean, this is part of the whole mess

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.527
that led to the circus-like
atmosphere of this trial.

00:10:59.527 --> 00:11:00.770
It was crazy.

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:01.603
- Mr. Schultz.

00:11:03.618 --> 00:11:05.210
- Good morning.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:06.400
My name is Richard Schultz.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:07.650
I'm an Assistant US Attorney

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.278
for the Southern District of Illinois.

00:11:09.278 --> 00:11:10.940
- All right, classic trope,

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:12.910
entering the well without permission

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.690
and going right up to the jury box.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:15.770
You have to ask for permission

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:17.820
or, obviously, the
bailiff will tackle you.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:19.550
There's actually one real life exchange

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:20.680
that was in the transcript

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.140
that didn't make it into the
movie that touches on this.

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:26.590
So William Kunstler is
the main defense lawyer,

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.540
and he was at the lectern,
but he was sort of leaning

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.310
on the lecture, kind of at
the side of the lectern,

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.240
and Judge Hoffman basically yelled at him,

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.920
saying that Mies van der
Rohe designed the lectern,

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.840
who's a famous architect,
and it's not allowed

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.280
to even lean on it.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:44.410
It's disrespectful.

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.450
Really, in this movie, we
see the tip of the iceberg

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:50.010
in terms of the judge's animosity towards,

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.730
not only the defense
counsel, but also the idea

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.799
that he was somehow racist
for chastising Bobby Seale

00:11:56.799 --> 00:12:01.030
and that he was constantly
the butt of the joke.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.020
Anytime there was laughter,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.554
he basically issued a contempt citation

00:12:03.554 --> 00:12:06.100
to the defense counsel
and he was always worried

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:06.933
that people were laughing at him

00:12:06.933 --> 00:12:10.170
and he was always worried that
people thought he was racist

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.800
and he went out of his way,
in sort of a racist way,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.090
to say that he wasn't racist.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.947
And he kept saying, you know,
"I've got black friends."

00:12:18.947 --> 00:12:22.100
I mean, it was just really,
really obvious stuff,

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.920
where he was trying to make a point

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.550
to show that he wasn't racist, and yet,

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:32.050
demonstrating that he was
at least mildly racist

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:33.000
for the time.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:33.833
- Mr. Schultz.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:38.903
- The radical left, that's all.

00:12:38.903 --> 00:12:41.520
They're the radical left
in different costumes.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.000
- That's probably improper
for an opening statement.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:45.650
- These defendants had a plan,

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:49.000
and a plan among two or
more people is a conspiracy.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.700
The defendants crossed state
lines to execute their plan.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.030
That's why we're in federal court.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:55.270
And the plan was to incite a riot.

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.453
And there's one thing you already know.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:12:59.620
They succeeded.

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:01.940
- Yeah, so there was a riot.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:03.170
There's no doubt about that.

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:06.700
But several investigations
showed that it was the police

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:08.240
that started the riot.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.960
There was a congressional

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.310
or Illinois state investigative report

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.290
called the Walker Report
that basically concluded

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:16.110
that it was the police,
not the protesters,

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:18.140
who started the riot in 1968.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.680
And it's interesting
because, here, there are

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:22.830
two separate types of
charges for the most part.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:26.370
One is a conspiracy charge
to cross state lines

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:27.690
to create a riot.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.210
The other charges were
just sort of general

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:31.820
incitement to riot charges that,

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:33.800
regardless of whether there
was a plan or conspiracy,

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.750
that they did cause a riot to happen.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:40.040
And the reason that you can
charge all these people together

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.850
and try them together
is the conspiracy charge

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.060
because, generally,
defendants have a right

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.040
to their own trial.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:49.050
But when the government
alleges a conspiracy,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.640
then you can lump them all together

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.050
and it's much easier to use statements

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.960
to show a plan amongst
this group of people,

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:57.350
which didn't really exist.

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.110
They are a really
disparate group of people

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.650
that, yes, got together for this protest

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.370
or what they called a love-in,
but it's very unlikely

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:07.270
that there was any kind
of thought-out plan

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:10.560
to actually incite a riot ahead of time.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.410
- Your Honor, I'm not with these guys.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:15.125
I never even met most of
them until the indictment.

00:14:15.125 --> 00:14:15.958
(gavel pounds)

00:14:15.958 --> 00:14:17.338
- We will have order.
- You got eight of us here

00:14:17.338 --> 00:14:18.792
and there're signs out there
- We'll have order.

00:14:18.792 --> 00:14:20.870
- that read "Free the Chicago 7."

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:22.490
I'm not with them.

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:23.940
- Mr. Marshall,
- This man says conspiracy.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:24.980
- will you seat Mr. Seale?
- I never even met most of 'em

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.060
until the indictment.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.680
And speaking frankly, the US Attorney

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.780
wanted a negro defendant
to scare the jury.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.240
I was thrown in to make
the group look scarier.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.105
I came to Chicago,
(gavel pounds repeatedly)

00:14:33.105 --> 00:14:33.938
I made a speech,

00:14:33.938 --> 00:14:36.600
- Mr. Seale-
- I had chicken pot pie,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.640
went to the airport and
I flew back to Oakland,

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.410
and that's why they call it the Chicago...

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:42.180
Get your hands off me.
(gavel pounds repeatedly)

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:46.940
- Charge Mr. Seale with one
count of contempt of court.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:51.130
- So, contempt of court, it
was a huge issue in this trial.

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:52.640
By the end of the trial,

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.550
there were 175 different
counts of contempt.

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:59.890
And between four of the
defendants and two of the lawyers,

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:02.710
some people were sentenced to
more than four years in jail

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:04.810
as a result of the contempt violations

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:06.110
that the judge handed down.

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:08.750
I mean, it's crazy that that
people would be sentenced

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:10.900
to four years in jail
for contempt of court

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.090
for things that happened in
court, not even the things

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.820
that they were alleged to
have done outside of court.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:18.080
Again, it contributed to this idea

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:19.610
that this was a political show trial.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:21.860
Rumors were that the prosecutors

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:24.220
really, really wanted
to get Judge Hoffman,

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:25.880
and they were ecstatic
when they did get him

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:27.750
because he was just
bringing down the hammer

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.180
on all of these different defendants.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:31.880
So the question is: What do you do

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:34.870
if you're the defense counsel
and the judge hates you

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:37.690
and is really taking it
out on you and your client?

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:39.340
Really, William Kunstler

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:40.970
probably should be the star of this show

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:43.610
because this movie is only
the tip of the iceberg

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.650
in terms of how much the
judge really hated Kunstler

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.900
and the rest of the defendants.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.710
Kunstler was fighting back
for months and months,

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:55.210
and as a result, he was issued
dozens of contempt citations

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:57.360
along with the defendants themselves.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.460
The only thing that you can
do is just set your record.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.700
You know that lot of these
rulings are not gonna stand,

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.140
that you're gonna be able to appeal it,

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.860
and if the judge is poisoning the well,

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.810
then you just keep setting the record.

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:09.820
You make your objections,

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.750
you state your grounds
for the the objections,

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.620
so that, when the trial is over

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:15.736
and if there is a conviction,

00:16:15.736 --> 00:16:17.550
you can go to the court of appeals

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:18.930
and point to these things.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:22.220
Frankly, this movie doesn't show

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:26.760
just how far Kunstler was
willing to go to fight this judge

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.000
who absolutely hated him and his clients.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:30.440
- What are you wearing?

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:33.070
- It's an homage to you, Your Honor.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.180
- Do you have clothes underneath there?

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:36.610
- Yes.

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:37.443
Hold on.

00:16:38.296 --> 00:16:39.430
Yes.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.023
- Take off the robes, please.

00:16:44.058 --> 00:16:45.200
(attendees laugh)

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.050
- Yeah, so this was one of the many antics

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:50.530
that Abbie Hoffman and Jerry Rubin pulled

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:51.610
during the course of this trial.

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:54.250
It is absolutely true that
they wore judicial robes,

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.800
trying to get the ire of Judge Hoffman.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:59.163
They were not wearing
police uniforms underneath,

00:16:59.163 --> 00:17:02.730
but in response to wearing
those robes, the judge did,

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:06.010
in fact, issue contempt
citations against them.

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:07.240
- Did you issue the permits?

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:08.561
- No, I did not.

00:17:08.561 --> 00:17:12.160
- And what, if anything,
did Abbie Hoffman say

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:14.240
when you denied the
request for the permit.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:16.520
- Mr. Stahl, you need
to understand something.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.910
There's gonna be a Festival
of Life in Grant Park

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:21.290
and it will be held during the convention.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.200
Bands will play rock music.

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:24.960
There will be public fornication,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.420
likely some of it with the wives

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:27.830
and mistresses of delegates.

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:29.810
Psychedelic, long-haired leftists

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:32.150
will consort with dope users.

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:32.983
And we're gonna insist

00:17:32.983 --> 00:17:34.780
that the next president
of the United States

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.310
stop sending our friends
to be slaughtered.

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:38.200
These things are gonna happen

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:39.710
whether you give us the permit or not.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:41.370
- The hotels will be
filled with delegates.

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:42.480
Where will people sleep?

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:43.550
- Some people will sleep in tents.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.110
Others will live frivolously.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.500
- Well how many people are coming here?

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:47.333
- A lot.

00:17:47.333 --> 00:17:48.166
- What's a lot?

00:17:48.166 --> 00:17:49.630
1,000?

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:50.463
2,000?

00:17:50.463 --> 00:17:51.720
- 10,000.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:52.910
- Jesus Christ.

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:53.752
- Right?

00:17:53.752 --> 00:17:54.670
(Devin chuckles)

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:57.440
- So, the permit issue
was a really big deal

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.970
because this is a really weird trial

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.180
in terms of the evidence
that was presented

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.540
and allowed to be presented.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.380
You're trying to show that
there was this conspiracy

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:08.856
ahead of time to cause a
riot, to incite violence.

00:18:08.856 --> 00:18:13.850
So a lot of what these
defendants said and did

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.880
goes to whether there was
a plan to create violence

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.210
or rioting before it actually happened.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:21.960
And you can imagine that
permitting is a big issue here.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.750
There's a lot of people, a
lot of historians that think

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:25.870
that, if the permits had been granted,

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:28.610
they would have had things like
sanitation and electricity.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.120
And the bands that they
had lined up to play

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:33.010
would've had a PA system,

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:35.410
which could have calmed the crowd down.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:37.760
And of course, the permits were rejected.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.550
- He said, "We must understand
that, as we go forth

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.210
to try to move the reprobate politicians,

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:45.186
- Your Honor?
- our cowardly Congress

00:18:45.186 --> 00:18:47.951
that jive, double-lip talk

00:18:47.951 --> 00:18:49.185
- Jive, double-lip talk
- and Nixon.

00:18:49.185 --> 00:18:50.018
- and Nixon.

00:18:50.018 --> 00:18:52.209
You make me sound like one funky cat.

00:18:52.209 --> 00:18:53.670
Thank you, sir.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.383
- Last warning, Mr. Seale.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:58.040
- Did he say anything else?

00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.876
- He said, "The revolution at this time

00:18:59.876 --> 00:19:04.876
is directly connected to
organized guns in force."

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.080
- No more questions.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:09.820
- Jive, double-lip talkin'.

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:11.810
Funky, funky cat.

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:14.420
- Would the defense like to
cross-examine the witness?

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:15.390
- Yes.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:16.410
I'm sitting here saying

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:17.913
that I would like to
cross-examine the witness.

00:19:17.913 --> 00:19:19.223
- Only lawyers can address

00:19:19.223 --> 00:19:21.450
the witness.
- My lawyer is Charles Garry.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:22.540
- I'm tired of hearing that.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:24.240
- Couldn't care less what you're tired of.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:25.743
- What did you say?

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:30.130
- I said it would be impossible for me

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:33.570
to care any less what you are tired of

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.540
and I demand to cross-examine the witness.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.990
- Sit in your chair and be quiet

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:43.120
and don't ever address this
court in that manner again.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.330
- So this incident actually happened.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.470
This is one of the more infamous incidents

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:52.020
in litigation history around this time.

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:53.072
It actually happened earlier.

00:19:53.072 --> 00:19:55.870
It was not related to the
death of Fred Hampton,

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:59.130
but obviously, Bobby Seale wanted a lawyer

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.370
and the court wasn't allowing him

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:03.220
to have his counsel of choice.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.780
After a number of disruptions,

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:07.700
Judge Hoffman really did put Bobby Seale

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:09.460
in chains, bound and gagged.

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:11.210
And what's not portrayed in this movie

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:12.980
is that it wasn't an isolated incident,

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:15.680
but he stayed in chains for days.

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:18.300
You can imagine that the
other defendants were pissed.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.780
The jury's sort of tainted at this point.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:22.610
This could've been its own movie.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.480
It sort of gets short shrift
in this particular movie,

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:29.380
but it actually happened and
it's not a shining moment

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.591
for the judicial system.

00:20:30.591 --> 00:20:32.500
- State your name.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:34.230
- [Ramsey] William Ramsey Clark.

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.880
- [Bailiff] Do you swear
that the testimony you give

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.210
will be the truth, the whole truth,

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:38.330
and nothing but the truth?

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:39.500
- [Ramsey] I do.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:41.670
- You would think that
this was pure movie magic.

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:43.980
It's made up that they were able to call

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:46.640
the former attorney general
for the United States

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:48.000
to be a witness in the trial.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.660
It actually happened.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:51.582
Interestingly, what this movie portrays,

00:20:51.582 --> 00:20:53.420
that the questions are asked,

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.780
he gives great answers for the defense

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:58.070
about the political nature of this trial,

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:00.820
and the judge does exclude the testimony.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:04.130
The jury never got to hear
the testimony of Ramsey Clark.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.040
This is interesting because,
generally, you would think that

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:08.830
what another police department did

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:11.160
or, in this case, the
attorney general's office did

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:13.169
isn't really probative
or relevant to the idea

00:21:13.169 --> 00:21:15.170
that these people are guilty or not.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:16.300
But there is a defense

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:19.130
of being singled out
for political reasons,

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:20.210
and I assume that that would've been

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:22.190
an affirmative defense
in this particular case.

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:23.620
What actually happens is that,

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:26.580
while Ramsey Clark did not
get to testify in this trial,

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.950
eventually, after the
appeal and a retrial,

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:33.360
he was able to testify later on.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.440
He testified, but the
jury never got to hear it.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.390
It's interesting what
they're not showing here

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:39.340
because there were hundreds of witnesses.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:42.278
And because of the defendants'
states of mind were at issue,

00:21:42.278 --> 00:21:44.100
they called a whole bunch of people

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:46.260
who participated in the protest,

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:48.580
witnesses like Alan Ginsburg, the poet,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.070
Dick Gregory, the comedian, folk singers,

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:53.050
Timothy Leary, the Harvard professor

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:55.400
who was famous for experimenting with LSD.

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.110
The testimony that they gave was insane.

00:21:57.110 --> 00:21:58.420
People sang songs.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:00.840
They chanted in the
middle of the courtroom.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.320
And this was the testimony in
front of the judge and jury.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:06.704
This was a bonkers trial
from start to finish.

00:22:06.704 --> 00:22:09.537
(whimsical music)

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:11.413
All right.

00:22:11.413 --> 00:22:13.530
Now it's time to give "The
Trial of the Chicago 7"

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:14.869
a grade for legal realism.

00:22:14.869 --> 00:22:17.460
(gavel pounds)

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:19.570
On the one hand, a lot of the dialogue

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:22.370
that's in this movie is almost verbatim

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:25.150
from the trial transcripts of
things that actually happened.

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.257
And sure, they're condensing things

00:22:27.257 --> 00:22:30.620
to fit it into a two-hour movie,
but it seems like they got

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:32.430
a lot of the period details correct.

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.714
On the other hand, there
are a few movie tropes,

00:22:34.714 --> 00:22:37.070
moving around the well,
but for the most part,

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:38.560
this is a pretty accurate movie,

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:40.080
both from the legal perspective

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:42.310
and the historical perspective as well.

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.190
So I'll give "The Trial of the Chicago 7"

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.670
an A- for accuracy.

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:48.085
This is about as accurate as
you'll get for a two-hour movie

00:22:48.085 --> 00:22:51.220
condensing six months worth of trial.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:52.190
To prepare for this review,

